WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.230
People are describing Avengers: Endgame as
a mind-blowing achievement in filmmaking,

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.580
a love letter to the fans, and a flawless
culmination of the entire Marvel Cinematic

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.860
Universe thus far.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.160
it's mostly been positivity all around for
Marvel Studios' 22nd movie "mostly" being

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.400
the operative word here.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.680
Reviews from critics are stellar, with the
film scoring Certified Fresh distinction on

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.319
Rotten Tomatoes less than 24 hours after the
review embargo lifted, but response from some

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:30.249
fans has been considerably less so… and
that's putting it lightly.

00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:35.329
If you want to find out what it is some people
are so mad about, no worries there are no

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:36.180
spoilers here.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.640
Apparently, many Marvel enthusiasts are angry
at Avengers: Endgame over one thing: it doesn't

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.320
feature a post-credits scene.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.640
Shocking news, we know, as it's customary
in the MCU for each installment to cap off

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.940
with at least one sequence after the credits
have rolled, whether it's a portentous tease

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:56.940
for movies to come or a humorous tag to leave
you laughing as the movie ends.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.000
I'm not that kind of doctor.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:00.740
I'm not a therapist.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:01.780
It's not my training.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.019
I don't have the—"

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:03.940
"What, the time?"

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.480
"Temperament."

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.500
While some movies in the series have had as
many as five little scenes during the credits,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.950
Avengers: Endgame has zero, much to the outrage
and confusion of some fans across the globe.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.770
If you waited for a thousand names to scroll
by and got nothing in return for it, you might

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.120
be a little ticked off too.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.570
"Hi — I'm Captain America, here to talk
to you about one of the most valuable traits

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:30.100
a soldier or student can have — patience."

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.960
After several sources confirmed that Endgame
goes without a single scene to cap things

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.170
off, and following the international debut
of the film earlier this week, fans took to

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.240
social media to lodge complaints and express
bewilderment.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.600
Along with a pair of frowning face emojis,
one Twitter user by the name of Roschelle

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.600
Apa said,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.420
"I'm actually emotional over the fact that
#AvengersEndGame won't have any post-credits

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:52.920
scene[s].

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.260
It's the end of an era, y'all."

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:59.060
Another admitted that he, quote, "waited like
an idiot" for 15 minutes after Endgame was

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.540
over, hoping for a mid- or post-credits scene
to play out on screen.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.230
He advised that everyone not make the same
mistake he did, though he did acknowledge

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.660
"The huge team that made the movie tho fire"

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.030
There were many more moviegoers who weren't
aware that no additional footage would screen

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:14.940
after Endgame.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.120
User Andy Belluna explained that an entire
theater full of people were irritated when

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.120
they made the realization:

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.820
"The movie has no post-credits scene, but
we didn't know, so the whole cinema sat still

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.780
and waited through the credits just to see
a black screen afterwards.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.020
Everyone was SO pissed and absolutely done
with their lives."

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.020
On the flip side, one fan who saw the film
already explained that no one in her screening

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.760
actually got up after the movie was over,
despite knowing that a post-credits scene

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.760
wasn't coming.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:40.540
She wrote on Twitter,

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.840
"As reported, there's no extra footage after
the credits (it's true), everybody knew it

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.270
and also the cinema staff told us, but nobody
left.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.980
I told them it's not that we don't trust you,
we're just too shook to move."

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.990
Other fans, perhaps having been burned by
missing out before, have sworn not to leave

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.700
their theater seats until the projector stops
running and the lights flicker back on.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.780
As one Twitter user put it,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.250
"Even if [the Russo brothers themselves] say
there are no post credit scenes, I'm not outta

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.940
my seat until the last line roll out on screen.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.100
What if it's a trap.."

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.130
They also appended the hashtag, "Things Marvel
Fans Are Used To."

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:17.360
Considering that Marvel Studios has previously
gone so far as to give cast members fake scripts

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.000
to keep spoilers at bay, manipulate trailers
to show shots not in the movie, and put actors

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.210
together for scenes that aren't real to fake
out paparazzi, fans' paranoia about being

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.880
tricked is somewhat well-founded.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.900
So it's no surprise that speculation has emerged
that Marvel could slip in a post-credits scene

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.670
to Endgame screenings starting on Thursday,
April 25, giving general audiences a little

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.900
something that early overseas showings and
press screenings didn't.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.300
This would somewhat mirror what 20th Century
Fox did with Deadpool: advanced screenings

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.720
did include a post-credits scene, but the
theatrical cut featured an extended sequence

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.140
that included the juicy information that Cable
would appear in the sequel.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.000
Additionally, the first Avengers movie also
included an extra shawarma scene that was

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.390
shot and edited into the movie after its world
premiere had already happened.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.000
And not to keep you guessing, but we are actually
not done shooting.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.560
We are shooting one more scene…" *gasps*
"...tonight."

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:13.340
Those precedents might be reason enough to
believe that fans heading to see Endgame as

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.220
early as Thursday night could experience what
others haven't yet.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.049
Of course, it's just hearsay for now, but
it's something to be aware of as you dart

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:23.200
out for the bathroom after three punishing
hours of superhero mayhem.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.240
If that doesn't happen and Avengers: Endgame
truly does continue to conclude right before

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.180
the credits, it wouldn't be all that bad.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:35.160
It could act as a reminder that Endgame is
different from any MCU movie that came before

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:35.660
it.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.400
As the saying goes, all good things must come
to an end even Marvel's post-credits-scene

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:40.920
tradition.

